2026-08-10T11:14:36.309Z [INFO] [rust:rmcp::service] Service initialized as client {"peer_info":"Some(ServerPeerInfo { protocol_version: ProtocolVersion(\"2025-11-25\"), capabilities: ServerCapabilities { experimental: None, extensions: None, logging: Some({}), completions: None, prompts: Some(PromptsCapability { list_changed: None }), resources: None, tools: Some(ToolsCapability { list_changed: None }) }, server_info: Some(Implementation { name: \"Bluebird\", title: None, version: \"1.0.0\", description: None, icons: None, website_url: None }), instructions: Some(\"Use this server's tools for code search and discovery. This server can find code by concept — not just exact keywords, searches all files in the repository even those not in your local workspace, and uses a pre-built index that is faster and won't time out. Built-in workspace tools (#codebase, #textSearch, #fileSearch) are only needed for verifying local file state or making edits.\\r\\n\\r\\nAVAILABLE TOOLS:\\r\\n- `code_search` — find code by keyword or by semantic concept\\r\\n- `code_read` — read file content, browse directories, and find files by name\\r\\n- `code_navigate` — explore code relationships: call graphs, class hierarchies, members, macro usage, type relationships (precise graph traversal when a graph index is available; approximated via keyword search otherwise — still useful, but less exhaustive)\\r\\n- `code_history` — commits, diffs, file history, pull requests\\r\\n- `project_search` — search wiki pages and work items (bugs, stories, tasks)\\r\\n- `metadata` — inspect the configured org/project/repo/branch (`connection_info`), list organizations the user has access to (`list_orgs`), and check repo indexing status (`repo_status`)\\r\\n\\r\\nEach tool uses a `method` parameter to select the operation. `code_navigate` is always available but approximate (keyword-based) for repos without a graph index.\\r\\n\\r\\nIMPORTANT — `original_user_question` parameter: Every tool requires an `original_user_question` parameter. Always pass the user's original question or prompt exactly as they wrote it. This enables context-aware responses.\\r\\n\\r\\nSCOPE — every tool call targets exactly ONE scope (an organization, optionally narrowed by\\r\\nproject / repository / branch). When the connection has a single configured scope, calls run\\r\\nagainst it automatically — you don't need to pass scope params. Pass `organization` (and\\r\\noptionally `project` / `repository` / `branch`) to target a different or broader scope, or to\\r\\npick one when several scopes are configured. Keyword `code_search` accepts a broad scope —\\r\\n`organization` alone searches the whole org, `organization`+`project` a whole project. Call\\r\\n`metadata` method=connection_info anytime to inspect the configured scope, or method=list_orgs\\r\\nto discover an organization.\\r\\n\\r\\nWORKFLOW (prefer hybrid methods when offered, fall back to keyword otherwise):\\r\\n1. If unsure of the configured scope, call `metadata` method=connection_info. A single configured scope is applied automatically — pass scope params only to target a different or narrower scope; with several configured, pass one per call.\\r\\n2. For concept/behavior queries, prefer `code_search` method=semantic — it finds code by meaning, not exact names. It targets one repo: pass `organization`+`project`+`repository` (or omit when the connection is already scoped to a repo). Fall back to method=keyword for cross-repo searches.\\r\\n3. When inspecting a class or function, use `code_read` method=content to read the source code.\\r\\n4. Use `code_navigate` to follow relationships rather than re-searching by name:\\r\\n   - CALLS / CALLED_BY — call graphs\\r\\n   - DERIVES_FROM / BASE_TYPE_OF — parent / child types\\r\\n   - IMPLEMENTS / IMPLEMENTED_BY — interface ↔ implementations\\r\\n   - MEMBER_FUNCTIONS / MEMBER_VARIABLES — class members\\r\\n   - NESTED_TYPES — inner classes\\r\\n   Chain calls recursively for transitive hierarchies (children of children).\\r\\n5. For feature-level questions, fan out in parallel:\\r\\n   - `code_search` for implementation — issue BOTH method=keyword and method=semantic (they rank differently)\\r\\n   - `code_history` for change history and PRs\\r\\n   - `project_search` method=wiki for design docs, SOPs, and rationale\\r\\n   - `project_search` method=work_items for planning context\\r\\n6. When the configured scope lacks a specific project/repo (or you've overridden it to broaden), start with a deliberately broad `code_search` keyword query and use the \\\"Results by project:\\\" / \\\"Results by repository:\\\" facet tables at the top of the response to pick the right project/repo before narrowing.\\r\\n\\r\\nQUERY SYNTAX (for `code_search` method=keyword):\\r\\n- Use code identifiers only — do NOT use natural language (\\\"how to\\\", \\\"implementation of\\\")\\r\\n- Multiple keywords are ANDed. Use 1-2 specific terms. Do NOT add generic words.\\r\\n  BAD:  query=\\\"AdvancedSecurity settings render\\\"\\r\\n  GOOD: query=\\\"AdvancedSecurity\\\"\\r\\n- Whole-token matching — `GetUser` won't match `GetUserProfileAsync`. Use the exact identifier, or a trailing wildcard for a prefix/family:\\r\\n  BAD:  query=\\\"GetUser\\\" (won't match GetUserProfileAsync)\\r\\n  GOOD: query=\\\"GetUserProfileAsync\\\" (exact) or query=\\\"GetUser*\\\" (prefix)\\r\\n- Don't know the exact name? Use method=semantic.\\r\\n- Use OR to broaden: \\\"ValidateToken OR AuthenticateUser\\\"\\r\\n- Code element prefixes (C#, C, C++, Java, VB.NET, Rust): class:, method:, def:, ref:, enum:, interface:, field:, prop:, macro:\\r\\n  Find definitions: query=\\\"class:MyClass\\\" or query=\\\"def:AnySymbol\\\"\\r\\n  Find usages: query=\\\"ref:MyMethod\\\"\\r\\n- Non-indexed languages (TypeScript, Python, Go, etc.): use the `ext` parameter\\r\\n\\r\\nANTI-PATTERNS:\\r\\n- Natural language queries return ZERO results: query=\\\"row level security implementation\\\" → 0 results\\r\\n- Too many keywords: query=\\\"auth token validate middleware\\\" → 0 results (over-constrained)\\r\\n- Vague terms in commit/wiki/work item search: \\\"recent changes\\\" → noise. Use specific identifiers.\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPLETENESS CHECK (before answering):\\r\\n✓ Did you try semantic search if it was available? Did you try keyword as well?\\r\\n✓ Did you read the relevant source? (`code_read` method=content)\\r\\n✓ Did you explore relationships if applicable? (`code_navigate` — callers, callees, inheritance, members)\\r\\n✓ For feature-level questions: did you check history, wiki, AND work items?\\r\\nIf any answer is no, go back and do it.\\r\\n\\r\\nGround answers in tool results with source snippets. Cite file/class/function names. The MCP index may lag local changes — verify with built-in tools when needed.\\n\\nCONTEXT DISCOVERY (required before first tool call):\\r\\nThe organization parameter is required on every tool call. To find it:\\r\\n1. If you have a local git clone, parse the git remote URL:\\r\\n   https://dev.azure.com/fabrikam/Northwind/_git/inventory-api\\r\\n   → organization=\\\"fabrikam\\\"\\r\\n2. If there is no local clone, call metadata.list_orgs to see organizations the user has access to, or ask the user.\"), meta: None })"}
