2026-08-10T11:14:40.702Z [INFO] [rust:rmcp::service] Service initialized as client {"peer_info":"Some(ServerPeerInfo { protocol_version: ProtocolVersion(\"2025-11-25\"), capabilities: ServerCapabilities { experimental: None, extensions: None, logging: None, completions: None, prompts: None, resources: None, tools: Some(ToolsCapability { list_changed: Some(true) }) }, server_info: Some(Implementation { name: \"enghub-mcp-server\", title: None, version: \"1.42.1\", description: None, icons: None, website_url: None }), instructions: Some(\"EngineeringHub MCP Server — tools for searching, reading, and giving feedback on eng.ms documentation.\\n\\nFEEDBACK WORKFLOW (important — do this automatically):\\nAfter fetching eng.ms pages and using them to help the user, submit feedback to help doc owners improve content quality.\\n1. Track which eng.ms pages you fetch during the conversation.\\n2. If the user indicates the content was helpful or resolved their issue, submit thumbs-up feedback via submit_feedback for each relevant page.\\n3. If the user indicates the content was unhelpful, outdated, incorrect, or confusing, submit thumbs-down feedback with a specific comment explaining why.\\n4. Infer feedback from the user's sentiment — you don't need them to explicitly say \\\"submit feedback\\\". Phrases like \\\"this worked perfectly\\\", \\\"these docs are outdated\\\", \\\"that page was useless\\\", or \\\"this is wrong\\\" are all feedback signals.\\n5. When submitting negative feedback, be specific in the comment: mention which sections are wrong, what information is outdated, or what steps are missing — not just \\\"bad docs\\\".\\n6. Submit feedback for ALL fetched pages that were relevant to the conversation, not just the last one.\\n7. When in doubt, ask the user if the documentation was helpful before ending the conversation.\"), meta: None })"}
